{# Transcribed from the static design mockup (upsell page). upsell.png → /assets/img/upsell.png.

   layouts/checkout.twig's <main> class is now an overridable `main_class` block
   (checkout.html's `lg:grid lg:grid-cols-2` is only its default); this page overrides
   it below with the mockup's own `flex flex-1 flex-col lg:flex-row`, and overrides
   `body_class` with the mockup's extra `flex min-h-screen flex-col` body classes
   (needed for footer pinning on this page, per upsell.html's <body> tag), so this page
   now matches upsell.html's layout exactly.

   Deliberate deviation from the static design: the shared partials/header-checkout.twig
   renders the logo as a link to home (`<a href="{{ url('/') }}">`), whereas the mockup's
   own upsell.html header uses a plain non-linking `<span>` (comment there: "one-time
   post-order upsell interstitial ... the only intended exits are the page's own
   actions"). Kept the logo linked here anyway, matching every other page — a harmless
   improvement over the mockup, not a defect.

   Every string and number here comes from the `upsell` view model. The
   mockup's own copy survives as the worked example in `config/upsells.php`, so a
   configured offer still renders the page that was drawn. Two departures from the
   mockup's *copy* (its markup is untouched):

   - the price is the model's decided figure and nothing else. The mockup printed
     "$8.99 / mo", but an upsell is one separate order placed once against the
     instrument the provider already holds — nothing in the model carries a
     recurrence, so a "/ mo" on the page that charges the card would be a billing
     promise the storefront cannot keep.
   - the mockup's "Cancel anytime. Free shipping included." footnote is configured per
     offer rather than written here, and renders only when a deployment sets it. Both the
     mockup's wording and the obvious correction to "One-time charge" are billing promises,
     and the second one is false against the offer this channel actually maps to.

   Both actions are real form posts ([8.7]) rather than the mockup's script-driven
   navigation. This is the one step in the funnel that charges a card, and with the
   mockup's `<button type="button">` pair the page did nothing at all with
   JavaScript off. theme/js/upsell.js is only a double-submit courtesy over the
   top of two working forms. `contents` on each form keeps the buttons themselves as
   the grid items of the mockup's `grid-cols-2` — a form cannot be a grid item here
   without changing the layout that was drawn, and the hidden CSRF input generates no
   box — the same trick checkout.twig uses to span its layout's columns.

   Anything this step has to *say* — a refused flood, an acceptance still in flight —
   is flashed and rendered by partials/cart-notice.twig, which the layout already
   includes above <main>. There is no notice on this page's model and no second place
   a message could appear. #}
{% extends 'layouts/checkout.twig' %}
{% set page_title = 'Exclusive Offer' %}
{% set page_description = 'AsterMD one-time offer: add ' ~ upsell.productName ~ ' to your confirmed order.' %}
{% block body_class %} flex min-h-screen flex-col{% endblock %}
{% block main_class %}flex flex-1 flex-col lg:flex-row{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

  {# An offer with no image renders no image column, rather than the mockup's
     coloured panel standing empty across 38% of a wide screen. The offer
     column is `flex-1` and takes the freed width on its own. #}
  {% if upsell.image %}
  <!-- ==================== PRODUCT IMAGE ==================== -->
  <div class="min-h-[240px] shrink-0 overflow-hidden bg-primary-soft lg:min-h-0 lg:w-[38%]">
    <img src="{{ upsell.image }}" alt="{{ upsell.productName }}" class="h-full w-full object-cover" />
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  <!-- ==================== EXCLUSIVE OFFER ==================== -->
  <div class="flex flex-1 items-center justify-center px-6 py-16 lg:px-20">
    <div class="w-full max-w-[500px]">
      <p class="flex items-center gap-2 text-base font-medium text-warning">
        <i data-lucide="circle-alert" class="size-6"></i>
        {{ upsell.eyebrow }}
      </p>

      <h1 class="font-heading mt-4 text-[28px] font-bold leading-tight text-heading sm:text-[32px]">{{ upsell.headline }}</h1>

      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-6 text-body">
        {{ upsell.body }}
      </p>

      <hr class="mt-6 border-border" />

      <div class="mt-6 flex items-start justify-between gap-4">
        <p class="text-base font-medium sm:text-lg text-heading">{{ upsell.productName }}</p>
        <p class="shrink-0 text-base font-medium sm:text-lg text-heading">{{ upsell.priceCents|money }}</p>
      </div>

      {# No bullets means no list: an empty <ul> is markup that says nothing and
         still takes the `mt-4` gap the mockup drew for three lines of it. #}
      {% if upsell.bullets is not empty %}
      <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-3">
        {% for bullet in upsell.bullets %}
        <li class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <i data-lucide="circle-check" class="size-5 shrink-0 text-primary"></i>
          <span class="text-base text-body">{{ bullet }}</span>
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
      {% endif %}

      {# Two forms, two endpoints, and nothing in either body but the CSRF token:
         which offer is being answered is the one at the journey's own cursor, so
         a stale page cannot name an add-on this journey never reached ([16.16]).
         Decline is first in tree order, as the mockup drew it — which also means
         Enter cannot be the keystroke that charges a card. #}
      <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <form method="post" action="{{ url(upsell.declinePath) }}" class="contents">
          <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="{{ csrf_token }}" />
          {# Confirms which offer is being refused, exactly as the accept form does.
             Declining costs no money but it does cost an offer, and a tab left open
             on an earlier one would otherwise skip whichever offer the cursor has
             since reached. #}
          <input type="hidden" name="upsell_key" value="{{ upsell.key }}" />
          <button type="submit" id="offer-decline" class="inline-flex w-full items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-sm border-2 border-border bg-background h-[41px] px-5 text-base font-medium sm:h-auto sm:py-4 text-heading transition-colors hover:border-primary hover:text-primary focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-primary disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-60">
            <i data-lucide="x" class="size-[14px]"></i>
            <span>{{ upsell.declineLabel }}</span>
          </button>
        </form>
        <form method="post" action="{{ url(upsell.acceptPath) }}" class="contents">
          <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="{{ csrf_token }}" />
          {# Which offer this page is showing. It confirms and can never choose:
             the server charges whatever the queue points at, and refuses when
             the two disagree — which is what stops a tab left open on an
             earlier offer from buying a later one the buyer never saw. #}
          <input type="hidden" name="upsell_key" value="{{ upsell.key }}" />
          <button type="submit" id="offer-accept" class="inline-flex w-full items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-primary h-[41px] px-5 text-base font-medium sm:h-auto sm:py-4 text-primary-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-primary-hover focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-primary-hover disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-60">
            <span>{{ upsell.acceptLabel }}</span>
            <i data-lucide="shopping-cart" class="size-[17px]"></i>
          </button>
        </form>
      </div>

      {# The mockup's "Cancel anytime" is a promise this storefront cannot keep, and the
         obvious correction to "One-time charge" is false against the provider offer this
         channel maps its products to — it comes back recurring. Whether an accepted offer
         bills again is the operator's fact, so the line is theirs to write and renders only
         when they have. #}
      {% if upsell.footnote %}
      <p class="mt-4 text-center text-sm text-muted">{{ upsell.footnote }}</p>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </div>

{% endblock %}
{% block scripts %}
{{ parent() }}
<script src="{{ asset('upsell.js') }}" defer></script>
{% endblock %}
